"Advanced Usage — Requests 2.34.2 documentation (https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/advanced/)\nciteturn33view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/user/advanced/\",\"lineno\":195}); Total lines: 678\nL26: Note, however, that method-level parameters will not be persisted across requests, even if using a session. This example will only send the cookies with the first request, but not the second:\nL27: \nL28:     s = requests.Session()\nL29: \nL30:     r = s.get('https://httpbin.org/cookies', cookies={'from-my': 'browser'})\nL31:     print(r.text)\nL32:     # '{\"cookies\": {\"from-my\": \"browser\"}}'\nL33: \nL34:     r = s.get('https://httpbin.org/cookies')\nL35:     print(r.text)\nL36:     # '{\"cookies\": {}}'\nL37: If you want to manually add cookies to your session, use the cite4†Cookie utility functions to manipulate cite5†`Session.cookies` .\nL38: \nL39: Sessions can also be used as context managers:\nL40: \nL41:     with requests.Session() as s:\nL42:         s.get('https://httpbin.org/cookies/set/sessioncookie/123456789')\nL43: \nL44: \nL45: This will make sure the session is closed as soon as the `with` block is exited, even if unhandled exceptions occurred.\nL46: \nL47: Remove a Value From a Dict Parameter\nL48: Sometimes you’ll want to omit session-level keys from a dict parameter. To do this, you simply set that key’s value to `None` in the method-level parameter. It will automatically be omitted.\nL49: \nL50: All values that are contained within a session are directly available to you. See the cite6†Session API Docs to learn more.\nL51: ## Request and Response Objectscite7†¶ L52: Whenever a call is made to `requests.get()` and friends, you are doing two major things. First, you are constructing a `Request` object which will be sent off to a server to request or query some resource. Second, a `Response` object is generated once Requests gets a response back from the server. The `Response` object contains all of the information returned by the server and also contains the `Request` object you created originally.\nL53: Here is a simple request to get some very important information from Wikipedia’s servers:\nL54: \nL55:     >>> r = requests.get('https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monty_Python')\nL56: If we want to access the headers the server sent back to us, we do this:\nL57: \nL58:     >>> r.headers\nL59:     {'content-length': '56170', 'x-content-type-options': 'nosniff', 'x-cache':\nL60:     'HIT from cp1006.eqiad.wmnet, MISS from cp1010.eqiad.wmnet', 'content-encoding':\nL61:     'gzip', 'age': '3080', 'content-language': 'en', 'vary': 'Accept-Encoding,Cookie',\nL62:     'server': 'Apache', 'last-modified': 'Wed, 13 Jun 2012 01:33:50 GMT',\nL63:     'connection': 'close', 'cache-control': 'private, s-maxage=0, max-age=0,\nL64:     must-revalidate', 'date': 'Thu, 14 Jun 2012 12:59:39 GMT', 'content-type':\nL65:     'text/html; charset=UTF-8', 'x-cache-lookup': 'HIT from cp1006.eqiad.wmnet:3128,\nL66:     MISS from cp1010.eqiad.wmnet:80'}\nL67: However, if we want to get the headers we sent the server, we simply access the request, and then the request’s headers:\nL68: \nL69:     >>> r.request.headers\nL70:     {'Accept-Encoding': 'identity, deflate, compress, gzip',\nL71:     'Accept': '*/*', 'User-Agent': 'python-requests/1.2.0'}\nL72: ## Prepared Requestscite8†¶ L73: Whenever you receive a cite9†`Response` object from an API call or a Session call, the `request` attribute is actually the `PreparedRequest` that was used. In some cases you may wish to do some extra work to the body or headers (or anything else really) before sending a request. The simple recipe for this is the following:\nL74: \nL75:     from requests import Request, Session\nL76: \nL77:     s = Session()\nL78: \nL79:     req = Request('POST', url, data=data, headers=headers)\nL80:     prepped = req.prepare()\nL81:     # do something with prepped.body\nL82:     prepped.body = 'No, I want exactly this as the body.'\nL83: \nL84:     # do something with prepped.headers\nL85:     del prepped.headers['Content-Type']\nL86: \nL87:     resp = s.send(prepped,\nL88:         stream=stream,\nL89:         verify=verify,\nL90:         proxies=proxies,\nL91:         cert=cert,\nL92:         timeout=timeout\nL93:     )\nL94: \nL95:     print(resp.status_code)\nL96: Since you are not doing anything special with the `Request` object, you prepare it immediately and modify the `PreparedRequest` object. You then send that with the other parameters you would have sent to `requests.*` or `Session.*`.\nL97: However, the above code will lose some of the advantages of having a Requests cite10†`Session` object. In particular, cite10†`Session` -level state such as cookies will not get applied to your request. To get a cite11†`PreparedRequest` with that state applied, replace the call to cite12†`Request.prepare()` with a call to cite13†`Session.prepare_request()` , like this:\nL98: \nL99:     from requests import Request, Session\nL100: \nL101:     s = Session()\nL102:     req = Request('GET',  url, data=data, headers=headers)\nL103:     prepped = s.prepare_request(req)\nL104: \nL105:     # do something with prepped.body\nL106:     prepped.body = 'Seriously, send exactly these bytes.'\nL107: \nL108:     # do something with prepped.headers\nL109:     prepped.headers['Keep-Dead'] = 'parrot'\nL110: \nL111:     resp = s.send(prepped,\nL112:         stream=stream,\nL113:         verify=verify,\nL114:         proxies=proxies,\nL115:         cert=cert,\nL116:         timeout=timeout\nL117:     )\nL118: \nL119:     print(resp.status_code)\nL120: When you are using the prepared request flow, keep in mind that it does not take into account the environment. This can cause problems if you are using environment variables to change the behaviour of requests. For example: Self-signed SSL certificates specified in `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE` will not be taken into account. As a result an `SSL: CERTIFICATE_VERIFY_FAILED` is thrown. You can get around this behaviour by explicitly merging the environment settings into your session:\nL121:     from requests import Request, Session\nL122: \nL123:     s = Session()\nL124:     req = Request('GET', url)\nL125: \nL126:     prepped = s.prepare_request(req)\nL127: \nL128:     # Merge environment settings into session\nL129:     settings = s.merge_environment_settings(prepped.url, {}, None, None, None)\nL130:     resp = s.send(prepped, **settings)\nL131: \nL132:     print(resp.status_code)\nL133: ## SSL Cert Verificationcite14†¶ L134: \nL135: Requests verifies SSL certificates for HTTPS requests, just like a web browser. By default, SSL verification is enabled, and Requests will throw a SSLError if it’s unable to verify the certificate:\nL136: \nL137:     >>> requests.get('https://requestb.in')\nL138:     requests.exceptions.SSLError: hostname 'requestb.in' doesn't match either of '*.herokuapp.com', 'herokuapp.com'\nL139: I don’t have SSL setup on this domain, so it throws an exception. Excellent. GitHub does though:\nL140: \nL141:     >>> requests.get('https://github.com')\nL142:     <Response [200]>\nL143: \nL144: \nL145: You can pass `verify` the path to a CA_BUNDLE file or directory with certificates of trusted CAs:\nL146: \nL147:     >>> requests.get('https://github.com', verify='/path/to/certfile')\nL148: \nL149: \nL150: or persistent:\nL151: \nL152:     s = requests.Session()\nL153:     s.verify = '/path/to/certfile'\nL154: \nL155: \nL156: Note\nL157: If `verify` is set to a path to a directory, the directory must have been processed using the `c_rehash` utility supplied with OpenSSL.\nL158: \nL159: This list of trusted CAs can also be specified through the `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE` environment variable. If `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE` is not set, `CURL_CA_BUNDLE` will be used as fallback.\nL160: \nL161: Requests can also ignore verifying the SSL certificate if you set `verify` to False:\nL162: \nL163:     >>> requests.get('https://kennethreitz.org', verify=False)\nL164:     <Response [200]>\nL165: Note that when `verify` is set to `False`, requests will accept any TLS certificate presented by the server, and will ignore hostname mismatches and/or expired certificates, which will make your application vulnerable to man-in-the-middle (MitM) attacks. Setting verify to `False` may be useful during local development or testing.\nL166: \nL167: By default, `verify` is set to True. Option `verify` only applies to host certs.\nL168: ## Client Side Certificatescite15†¶ L169: \nL170: You can also specify a local cert to use as client side certificate, as a single file (containing the private key and the certificate) or as a tuple of both files’ paths:\nL171: \nL172:     >>> requests.get('https://kennethreitz.org', cert=('/path/client.cert', '/path/client.key'))\nL173:     <Response [200]>\nL174: \nL175: \nL176: or persistent:\nL177: \nL178:     s = requests.Session()\nL179:     s.cert = '/path/client.cert'\nL180: If you specify a wrong path or an invalid cert, you’ll get a SSLError:\nL181: \nL182:     >>> requests.get('https://kennethreitz.org', cert='/wrong_path/client.pem')\nL183:     SSLError: [Errno 336265225] _ssl.c:347: error:140B0009:SSL routines:SSL_CTX_use_PrivateKey_file:PEM lib\nL184: \nL185: \nL186: Warning\nL187: \nL188: The private key to your local certificate must be unencrypted. Currently, Requests does not support using encrypted keys.\nL189: ## CA Certificatescite16†¶ L190: \nL191: Requests uses certificates from the package cite17†certifi†certifiio.readthedocs.io . This allows for users to update their trusted certificates without changing the version of Requests.\nL192: Before version 2.16, Requests bundled a set of root CAs that it trusted, sourced from the cite18†Mozilla trust store†hg.mozilla.org . The certificates were only updated once for each Requests version. When `certifi` was not installed, this led to extremely out-of-date certificate bundles when using significantly older versions of Requests.\nL193: \nL194: For the sake of security we recommend upgrading certifi frequently!\nL195: ## Body Content Workflowcite19†¶ L196: \nL197: By default, when you make a request, the body of the response is downloaded immediately. You can override this behaviour and defer downloading the response body until you access the cite20†`Response.content` attribute with the `stream` parameter:\nL198: \nL199:     tarball_url = 'https://github.com/psf/requests/tarball/main'\nL200:     r = requests.get(tarball_url, stream=True)\nL201: At this point only the response headers have been downloaded and the connection remains open, hence allowing us to make content retrieval conditional:\nL202: \nL203:     if int(r.headers['content-length']) < TOO_LONG:\nL204:       content = r.content\nL205:       ...\nL206: You can further control the workflow by use of the cite21†`Response.iter_content()` and cite22†`Response.iter_lines()` methods. Alternatively, you can read the undecoded body from the underlying urllib3 cite23†`urllib3.HTTPResponse`†urllib3.readthedocs.io at cite24†`Response.raw` .\nL207: If you set `stream` to `True` when making a request, Requests cannot release the connection back to the pool unless you consume all the data or call cite25†`Response.close` . This can lead to inefficiency with connections. If you find yourself partially reading request bodies (or not reading them at all) while using `stream=True`, you should make the request within a `with` statement to ensure it’s always closed:\nL208: \nL209:     with requests.get('https://httpbin.org/get', stream=True) as r:\nL210:         # Do things with the response here.\nL211: ## Keep-Alivecite26†¶ L212: \nL213: Excellent news — thanks to urllib3, keep-alive is 100% automatic within a session! Any requests that you make within a session will automatically reuse the appropriate connection!\nL214: \nL215: Note that connections are only released back to the pool for reuse once all body data has been read; be sure to either set `stream` to `False` or read the `content` property of the `Response` object.\nL216: ## Streaming Uploadscite27†¶ L217: \nL218: Requests supports streaming uploads, which allow you to send large streams or files without reading them into memory. To stream and upload, simply provide a file-like object for your body:\nL219: \nL220:     with open('massive-body', 'rb') as f:\nL221:         requests.post('http://some.url/streamed', data=f)\nL222: \nL223: \nL224: Warning\nL225: It is strongly recommended that you open files in cite28†binary mode†docs.python.org . This is because Requests may attempt to provide the `Content-Length` header for you, and if it does this value will be set to the number of bytes in the file. Errors may occur if you open the file in text mode.\nL226: ## Chunk-Encoded Requestscite29†¶ L227: \nL228: Requests also supports Chunked transfer encoding for outgoing and incoming requests. To send a chunk-encoded request, simply provide a generator (or any iterator without a length) for your body:\nL229: \nL230:     def gen():\nL231:         yield 'hi'\nL232:         yield 'there'\nL233: \nL234:     requests.post('http://some.url/chunked', data=gen())\nL235: For chunked encoded responses, it’s best to iterate over the data using cite21†`Response.iter_content()` . In an ideal situation you’ll have set `stream=True` on the request, in which case you can iterate chunk-by-chunk by calling `iter_content` with a `chunk_size` parameter of `None`. If you want to set a maximum size of the chunk, you can set a `chunk_size` parameter to any integer.\nL236: ## POST Multiple Multipart-Encoded Filescite30†¶ L237: \nL238: You can send multiple files in one request. For example, suppose you want to upload image files to an HTML form with a multiple file field ‘images’:\nL239: \nL240:     <input type=\"file\" name=\"images\" multiple=\"true\" required=\"true\"/>\nL241: To do that, just set files to a list of tuples of `(form_field_name, file_info)`:\nL242: \nL243:     >>> url = 'https://httpbin.org/post'\nL244:     >>> multiple_files = [\nL245:     ...     ('images', ('foo.png', open('foo.png', 'rb'), 'image/png')),\nL246:     ...     ('images', ('bar.png', open('bar.png', 'rb'), 'image/png'))]\nL247:     >>> r = requests.post(url, files=multiple_files)\nL248:     >>> r.text\nL249:     {\nL250:       ...\nL251:       'files': {'images': 'data:image/png;base64,iVBORw ....'}\nL252:       'Content-Type': 'multipart/form-data; boundary=3131623adb2043caaeb5538cc7aa0b3a',\nL253:       ...\nL254:     }\nL255: Warning\nL256: \nL257: It is strongly recommended that you open files in cite28†binary mode†docs.python.org . This is because Requests may attempt to provide the `Content-Length` header for you, and if it does this value will be set to the number of bytes in the file. Errors may occur if you open the file in text mode.\nL258: ## Event Hookscite31†¶ L259: \nL260: Requests has a hook system that you can use to manipulate portions of the request process, or signal event handling.\nL261: \nL262: Available hooks:\nL263: \nL264: `response`:\nL265: \nL266: \nL267: The response generated from a Request.\nL268: \nL269: You can assign a hook function on a per-request basis by passing a `{hook_name: callback_function}` dictionary to the `hooks` request parameter:\nL270: \nL271:     hooks={'response': print_url}\nL272: That `callback_function` will receive a chunk of data as its first argument.\nL273: \nL274:     def print_url(r, *args, **kwargs):\nL275:         print(r.url)\nL276: \nL277: \nL278: Your callback function must handle its own exceptions. Any unhandled exception won’t be passed silently and thus should be handled by the code calling Requests.\nL279: If the callback function returns a value, it is assumed that it is to replace the data that was passed in. If the function doesn’t return anything, nothing else is affected.\nL280: \nL281:     def record_hook(r, *args, **kwargs):\nL282:         r.hook_called = True\nL283:         return r\nL284: \nL285: \nL286: Let’s print some request method arguments at runtime:\nL287: \nL288:     >>> requests.get('https://httpbin.org/', hooks={'response': print_url})\nL289:     https://httpbin.org/\nL290:     <Response [200]>\nL291: You can add multiple hooks to a single request. Let’s call two hooks at once:\nL292: \nL293:     >>> r = requests.get('https://httpbin.org/', hooks={'response': [print_url, record_hook]})\nL294:     https://httpbin.org/\nL295:     >>> r.hook_called\nL296:     True\nL297: You can also add hooks to a `Session` instance. Any hooks you add will then be called on every request made to the session. For example:\nL298: \nL299:     >>> s = requests.Session()\nL300:     >>> s.hooks['response'].append(print_url)\nL301:     >>> s.get('https://httpbin.org/')\nL302:      https://httpbin.org/\nL303:      <Response [200]>\nL304: \nL305: \nL306: A `Session` can have multiple hooks, which will be called in the order they are added.\nL307: ## Custom Authenticationcite32†¶ L308: \nL309: Requests allows you to specify your own authentication mechanism.\nL310: \nL311: Any callable which is passed as the `auth` argument to a request method will have the opportunity to modify the request before it is dispatched.\nL312: \nL313: Authentication implementations are subclasses of cite33†`AuthBase` , and are easy to define. Requests provides two common authentication scheme implementations in `requests.auth`: cite34†`HTTPBasicAuth` and cite35†`HTTPDigestAuth` .\nL314: Let’s pretend that we have a web service that will only respond if the `X-Pizza` header is set to a password value. Unlikely, but just go with it.\nL315: \nL316:     from requests.auth import AuthBase\nL317: \nL318:     class PizzaAuth(AuthBase):\nL319:         \"\"\"Attaches HTTP Pizza Authentication to the given Request object.\"\"\"\nL320:         def __init__(self, username):\nL321:             # setup any auth-related data here\nL322:             self.username = username\nL323: \nL324:         def __call__(self, r):\nL325:             # modify and return the request\nL326:             r.headers['X-Pizza'] = self.username\nL327:             return r\nL328: Then, we can make a request using our Pizza Auth:\nL329: \nL330:     >>> requests.get('http://pizzabin.org/admin', auth=PizzaAuth('kenneth'))\nL331:     <Response [200]>\nL332: ## Streaming Requestscite36†¶ L333: With cite22†`Response.iter_lines()` you can easily iterate over streaming APIs such as the cite37†Twitter Streaming API†dev.twitter.com . Simply set `stream` to `True` and iterate over the response with cite22†`iter_lines` :\nL334: \nL335:     import json\nL336:     import requests\nL337: \nL338:     r = requests.get('https://httpbin.org/stream/20', stream=True)\nL339: \nL340:     for line in r.iter_lines():\nL341: \nL342:         # filter out keep-alive new lines\nL343:         if line:\nL344:             decoded_line = line.decode('utf-8')\nL345:             print(json.loads(decoded_line))\nL346: When using decode_unicode=True with cite22†`Response.iter_lines()` or cite21†`Response.iter_content()` , you’ll want to provide a fallback encoding in the event the server doesn’t provide one:\nL347: \nL348:     r = requests.get('https://httpbin.org/stream/20', stream=True)\nL349: \nL350:     if r.encoding is None:\nL351:         r.encoding = 'utf-8'\nL352: \nL353:     for line in r.iter_lines(decode_unicode=True):\nL354:         if line:\nL355:             print(json.loads(line))\nL356: \nL357: \nL358: Warning\nL359: cite22†`iter_lines` is not reentrant safe. Calling this method multiple times causes some of the received data being lost. In case you need to call it from multiple places, use the resulting iterator object instead:\nL360: \nL361:     lines = r.iter_lines()\nL362:     # Save the first line for later or just skip it\nL363: \nL364:     first_line = next(lines)\nL365: \nL366:     for line in lines:\nL367:         print(line)\nL368: ## Proxiescite38†¶ L369: \nL370: If you need to use a proxy, you can configure individual requests with the `proxies` argument to any request method:\nL371: \nL372:     import requests\nL373: \nL374:     proxies = {\nL375:       'http': 'http://10.10.1.10:3128',\nL376:       'https': 'http://10.10.1.10:1080',\nL377:     }\nL378: \nL379:     requests.get('http://example.org', proxies=proxies)\nL380: Alternatively you can configure it once for an entire cite10†`Session` :\nL381: \nL382:     import requests\nL383: \nL384:     proxies = {\nL385:       'http': 'http://10.10.1.10:3128',\nL386:       'https': 'http://10.10.1.10:1080',\nL387:     }\nL388:     session = requests.Session()\nL389:     session.proxies.update(proxies)\nL390: \nL391:     session.get('http://example.org')\nL392: \nL393: \nL394: Warning\nL395: Setting `session.proxies` may behave differently than expected. Values provided will be overwritten by environmental proxies (those returned by cite39†urllib.request.getproxies†docs.python.org ). To ensure the use of proxies in the presence of environmental proxies, explicitly specify the `proxies` argument on all individual requests as initially explained above.\nL396: \nL397: See cite40†#2018†github.com for details.\nL398: When the proxies configuration is not overridden per request as shown above, Requests relies on the proxy configuration defined by standard environment variables `http_proxy`, `https_proxy`, `no_proxy`, and `all_proxy`. Uppercase variants of these variables are also supported. You can therefore set them to configure Requests (only set the ones relevant to your needs):\nL399: \nL400:     $ export HTTP_PROXY=\"http://10.10.1.10:3128\"\nL401:     $ export HTTPS_PROXY=\"http://10.10.1.10:1080\"\nL402:     $ export ALL_PROXY=\"socks5://10.10.1.10:3434\"\nL403: \nL404:     $ python\nL405:     >>> import requests\nL406:     >>> requests.get('http://example.org')\nL407: To use HTTP Basic Auth with your proxy, use the http://user:password@host/ syntax in any of the above configuration entries:\nL408: \nL409:     $ export HTTPS_PROXY=\"http://user:pass@10.10.1.10:1080\"\nL410: \nL411:     $ python\nL412:     >>> proxies = {'http': 'http://user:pass@10.10.1.10:3128/'}\nL413: \nL414: \nL415: Warning\nL416: \nL417: Storing sensitive username and password information in an environment variable or a version-controlled file is a security risk and is highly discouraged.\nL418: To give a proxy for a specific scheme and host, use the scheme://hostname form for the key. This will match for any request to the given scheme and exact hostname.\nL419: \nL420:     proxies = {'http://10.20.1.128': 'http://10.10.1.10:5323'}\nL421: \nL422: \nL423: Note that proxy URLs must include the scheme.\nL424: Finally, note that using a proxy for https connections typically requires your local machine to trust the proxy’s root certificate. By default the list of certificates trusted by Requests can be found with:\nL425: \nL426:     from requests.utils import DEFAULT_CA_BUNDLE_PATH\nL427:     print(DEFAULT_CA_BUNDLE_PATH)\nL428: You override this default certificate bundle by setting the `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE` (or `CURL_CA_BUNDLE`) environment variable to another file path:\nL429: \nL430:     $ export REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE=\"/usr/local/myproxy_info/cacert.pem\"\nL431:     $ export https_proxy=\"http://10.10.1.10:1080\"\nL432: \nL433:     $ python\nL434:     >>> import requests\nL435:     >>> requests.get('https://example.org')\nL436: ### SOCKScite41†¶ L437: \nL438: New in version 2.10.0.\nL439: \nL440: In addition to basic HTTP proxies, Requests also supports proxies using the SOCKS protocol. This is an optional feature that requires that additional third-party libraries be installed before use.\nL441: \nL442: You can get the dependencies for this feature from `pip`:\nL443: \nL444:     $ python -m pip install 'requests[socks]'\nL445: Once you’ve installed those dependencies, using a SOCKS proxy is just as easy as using a HTTP one:\nL446: \nL447:     proxies = {\nL448:         'http': 'socks5://user:pass@host:port',\nL449:         'https': 'socks5://user:pass@host:port'\nL450:     }\nL451: Using the scheme `socks5` causes the DNS resolution to happen on the client, rather than on the proxy server. This is in line with curl, which uses the scheme to decide whether to do the DNS resolution on the client or proxy. If you want to resolve the domains on the proxy server, use `socks5h` as the scheme.\nL452: ## Compliancecite42†¶ L453: \nL454: Requests is intended to be compliant with all relevant specifications and RFCs where that compliance will not cause difficulties for users. This attention to the specification can lead to some behaviour that may seem unusual to those not familiar with the relevant specification.\nL455: ### Encodingscite43†¶ L456: \nL457: When you receive a response, Requests makes a guess at the encoding to use for decoding the response when you access the cite44†`Response.text` attribute. Requests will first check for an encoding in the HTTP header, and if none is present, will use cite45†charset_normalizer†pypi.org or cite46†chardet†github.com to attempt to guess the encoding.\nL458: If `chardet` is installed, `requests` uses it, however for python3 `chardet` is no longer a mandatory dependency. The `chardet` library is an LGPL-licenced dependency and some users of requests cannot depend on mandatory LGPL-licensed dependencies.\nL459: \nL460: When you install `requests` without specifying `[use_chardet_on_py3]` extra, and `chardet` is not already installed, `requests` uses `charset-normalizer` (MIT-licensed) to guess the encoding.\nL461: The only time Requests will not guess the encoding is if no explicit charset is present in the HTTP headers and the `Content-Type` header contains `text`. In this situation, cite47†RFC 2616†www.w3.org specifies that the default charset must be `ISO-8859-1`. Requests follows the specification in this case. If you require a different encoding, you can manually set the cite48†`Response.encoding` property, or use the raw cite20†`Response.content` .\nL462: ## HTTP Verbscite49†¶ L463: \nL464: Requests provides access to almost the full range of HTTP verbs: GET, OPTIONS, HEAD, POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE. The following provides detailed examples of using these various verbs in Requests, using the GitHub API.\nL465: We will begin with the verb most commonly used: GET. HTTP GET is an idempotent method that returns a resource from a given URL. As a result, it is the verb you ought to use when attempting to retrieve data from a web location. An example usage would be attempting to get information about a specific commit from GitHub. Suppose we wanted commit `a050faf` on Requests. We would get it like so:\nL466: \nL467:     >>> import requests\nL468:     >>> r = requests.get('https://api.github.com/repos/psf/requests/git/commits/a050faf084662f3a352dd1a941f2c7c9f886d4ad')\nL469: We should confirm that GitHub responded correctly. If it has, we want to work out what type of content it is. Do this like so:\nL470: \nL471:     >>> if r.status_code == requests.codes.ok:\nL472:     ...     print(r.headers['content-type'])\nL473:     ...\nL474:     application/json; charset=utf-8\nL475: So, GitHub returns JSON. That’s great, we can use the cite50†`r.json` method to parse it into Python objects.\nL476: \nL477:     >>> commit_data = r.json()\nL478: \nL479:     >>> print(commit_data.keys())\nL480:     ['committer', 'author', 'url', 'tree', 'sha', 'parents', 'message']\nL481: \nL482:     >>> print(commit_data['committer'])\nL483:     {'date': '2012-05-10T11:10:50-07:00', 'email': 'me@kennethreitz.com', 'name': 'Kenneth Reitz'}\nL484: \nL485:     >>> print(commit_data['message'])\nL486:     makin' history\nL487: So far, so simple. Well, let’s investigate the GitHub API a little bit. Now, we could look at the documentation, but we might have a little more fun if we use Requests instead. We can take advantage of the Requests OPTIONS verb to see what kinds of HTTP methods are supported on the url we just used.\nL488: \nL489:     >>> verbs = requests.options(r.url)\nL490:     >>> verbs.status_code\nL491:     500\nL492: Uh, what? That’s unhelpful! Turns out GitHub, like many API providers, don’t actually implement the OPTIONS method. This is an annoying oversight, but it’s OK, we can just use the boring documentation. If GitHub had correctly implemented OPTIONS, however, they should return the allowed methods in the headers, e.g.\nL493: \nL494:     >>> verbs = requests.options('http://a-good-website.com/api/cats')\nL495:     >>> print(verbs.headers['allow'])\nL496:     GET,HEAD,POST,OPTIONS\nL497: Turning to the documentation, we see that the only other method allowed for commits is POST, which creates a new commit. As we’re using the Requests repo, we should probably avoid making ham-handed POSTS to it. Instead, let’s play with the Issues feature of GitHub.\nL498: This documentation was added in response to cite51†Issue #482†github.com . Given that this issue already exists, we will use it as an example. Let’s start by getting it.\nL499: \nL500:     >>> r = requests.get('https://api.github.com/repos/psf/requests/issues/482')\nL501:     >>> r.status_code\nL502:     200\nL503: \nL504:     >>> issue = json.loads(r.text)\nL505: \nL506:     >>> print(issue['title'])\nL507:     Feature any http verb in docs\nL508: \nL509:     >>> print(issue['comments'])\nL510:     3\nL511: Cool, we have three comments. Let’s take a look at the last of them.\nL512: \nL513:     >>> r = requests.get(r.url + '/comments')\nL514:     >>> r.status_code\nL515:     200\nL516: \nL517:     >>> comments = r.json()\nL518: \nL519:     >>> print(comments[0].keys())\nL520:     ['body', 'url', 'created_at', 'updated_at', 'user', 'id']\nL521: \nL522:     >>> print(comments[2]['body'])\nL523:     Probably in the \"advanced\" section\nL524: Well, that seems like a silly place. Let’s post a comment telling the poster that he’s silly. Who is the poster, anyway?\nL525: \nL526:     >>> print(comments[2]['user']['login'])\nL527:     kennethreitz\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAdvanced Usage - urllib3 2.8.0 documentation (https://urllib3.readthedocs.io/en/stable/advanced-usage.html)\nciteturn33view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://urllib3.readthedocs.io/en/stable/advanced-usage.html#streaming-and-i-o\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 413\n"
